<h2>Question</h2>
<p>
  The <code>/consolidate</code> pass can prove that two open backlog items are genuinely one job — one item's
  scope sits entirely inside another's. When it does, the backlog has <strong>no honest way to retire the
  absorbed one</strong>. <code>resolve</code> means <em>delivered</em> and feeds the burndown, so closing a
  never-built duplicate books points nobody earned. A park must reduce to a real structural gate
  (<code>blockedBy</code>, <code>humanGate</code>, <code>platform-gated</code>, <code>maturityGated</code>) and
  "another item already covers this" is none of those. And <code>supersededBy</code> exists, but its pointer
  space is research-topic ids, not backlog ids. So the fold outcome is <strong>reported and never
  executed</strong>.
</p>
<p>
  Decision <a href="/backlog/2982-how-a-folded-duplicate-backlog-item-retires-foldedinto-point/">#2982</a> asks
  two things. Does a proven-contained duplicate get a retirement state at all, or does it stay open and
  cross-referenced? And if it retires, is the marker a new <code>status</code> value or a field on an item that
  stays <code>open</code>?
</p>

<h2>Prior art — five trackers, one convergent answer</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Tracker</th>
      <th>Terminal state for a duplicate</th>
      <th>Pointer to the survivor</th>
      <th>Counted as delivered?</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Bugzilla</strong></td>
      <td><code>RESOLVED</code> + resolution <code>DUPLICATE</code></td>
      <td><code>dupe_of</code> — a first-class bug-id field on the bug, not a comment</td>
      <td>No — <code>DUPLICATE</code> is a distinct resolution from <code>FIXED</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Jira</strong></td>
      <td>Closed + Resolution <code>Duplicate</code></td>
      <td>a separate <code>duplicates</code> issue-link type — a second, independent step; Jira attaches no
      behaviour to the link's name</td>
      <td>No — the standard practice is to filter <code>resolution != Duplicate</code> out of reports and epic
      progress</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>GitHub Issues</strong></td>
      <td>closed with <code>state_reason: duplicate</code> — a <em>third</em> close reason added Dec 2024,
      alongside <code>completed</code> and <code>not_planned</code></td>
      <td>a "marked as a duplicate" timeline event plus a note rendered at the top of the issue explaining why
      it closed</td>
      <td>No — deliberately distinct from <code>completed</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>GitLab</strong></td>
      <td>closed via the <code>/duplicate #id</code> quick action</td>
      <td>a duplicate-of relation plus a related-issue link</td>
      <td>No</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Linear</strong></td>
      <td>moved into the reserved <code>Duplicate</code> status, whose <em>type</em> is <strong>Canceled</strong>
      — not Done</td>
      <td>a duplicate relation to the canonical issue; attachments and customer requests migrate to the
      survivor</td>
      <td>No — the Canceled type is separate from Done everywhere Done is counted</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3>Finding 1 — the non-delivered close is universal (5/5); "stay open and cross-ref" is 0/5</h3>
<p>
  No surveyed tracker models a proven duplicate by leaving it in the working set with a "see also" link. All
  five move it out of the active set and mark <em>why</em>. The consistency carries weight precisely because
  the five disagree about almost everything else in their lifecycle models — resolutions versus close-reasons
  versus status types — yet converge here.
</p>
<p>
  This also corrects the framing that this repo's standing <em>Review before adding (dedup)</em> rule points
  the other way. Read literally, that rule says <strong>"if an item already covers the idea, <em>extend</em>
  it rather than adding a near-duplicate sibling"</strong>, and reserves <em>cross-reference instead of
  merging</em> for <strong>parallel tracks that look similar but are distinct</strong>. It governs the
  <em>adjacent</em> case, not the <em>contained</em> one — so it is not a counterweight to a fold.
</p>

<h3>Finding 2 — the close reason is a positive enum marker, not an inference (4/5)</h3>
<p>
  Bugzilla's <code>DUPLICATE</code> resolution, Jira's <code>Duplicate</code> resolution, GitHub's
  <code>state_reason: duplicate</code> and Linear's reserved <code>Duplicate</code> status each carry an
  explicit marker of <em>why</em> the item left, paired with a <em>separate</em> pointer to the survivor.
  Nobody encodes the survivor inside the enum, and nobody relies on the pointer alone to mean "closed as
  duplicate." Only GitLab infers duplicate-ness purely from the presence of a relation — and GitLab is also
  the one tracker with a long-running complaint about the resulting state.
</p>

<h3>Finding 3 — the fold must be reversible; this is where prior art records a real defect</h3>
<p>
  GitLab's <code>/duplicate</code> has no inverse. The issue "Allow removal of issues marked as duplicate"
  (<code>gitlab-org/gitlab#233050</code>) has been open for years: once marked, the relation cannot be
  unlinked, so an accidental fold is permanent and the issue is thereafter always displayed as <em>Closed
  (duplicated)</em>. This is the concrete, observed form of the "an over-eager fold silently removes work"
  risk — and it argues for shipping the <strong>unfold</strong> path in the same slice as the fold path,
  rather than as a follow-up.
</p>

<h3>Finding 4 — supersession-as-a-pointer is already ratified here, for a different home</h3>
<p>
  This repo's existing retirement convention (the reference-retirement doc, #584) explicitly rejects an enum:
  death and supersession are "distinct, independently-optional facts … two markers, not one <code>status</code>
  enum — an enum is mutually exclusive and so can't represent the 'dead and superseded' case." Its scope is
  this repo's <em>reference data</em>, and its stated ambition is uniformity — "the homes differ in
  <em>container</em>, not in the retirement <em>concept</em>" — so it has to be reconciled rather than ignored.
</p>
<p>
  The reconciliation is that the objection is <strong>scope-bound</strong>. It exists because a reference can
  be <em>both</em> dead <em>and</em> superseded, so one enum slot cannot hold both facts. A backlog item cannot
  be both <em>delivered</em> and <em>folded</em> — those are genuinely exclusive — so the objection does not
  reach this case. What does carry over is the <em>field-set shape</em>: a death triplet
  (<code>retired</code> + <code>retiredDate</code> + <code>retiredReason</code>) alongside a supersession
  pointer.
</p>

<h2>Scoring the shape against this repo's own machinery</h2>
<p>
  The status-versus-field sub-call is not a free choice; the loader and the gate already price it.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>A distinct status drops out of every selection surface for free.</strong> The tier derivation
  returns <code>undefined</code> for any non-<code>open</code> item, and the readiness engine filters its whole
  selection to <code>status === 'open'</code> — so tier, batchable, sliceable, splittable and the open leverage
  graph all exclude a folded item with zero new code.</li>
  <li><strong>A field on an item that stays <code>open</code> must be excluded by hand at each of those
  sites</strong>, and it also trips the call-to-action invariant: every open item with no next-action pill is a
  hard gate error.</li>
  <li><strong>The burndown needs one change either way.</strong> Its "done" key is
  <code>status === 'resolved' &amp;&amp; dateResolved</code>, while a sized item enters <em>scope</em> purely
  from its opened date — so a folded item must be excluded from the sized set, or its points sit in remaining
  scope forever. Excluding rather than fabricating a delivered date is the repo's own
  <em>exclude, never fabricate, at a lossy boundary</em> rule.</li>
  <li><strong>There is precedent for minting a status value</strong>: <code>preparing</code> was added for
  exactly this reason — non-open and in-flight, dropping from selection like <code>active</code> but distinct
  on the board.</li>
  <li><strong>There is no frontmatter-key whitelist for backlog items</strong>; an unknown key is silently
  spread onto the item. That is a risk rather than a convenience — <code>relatedTo</code> (232 items),
  <code>relatedItems</code> and <code>blocks</code> already exist on disk as inter-item pointers with zero
  validation and zero rendering. A bare <code>foldedInto:</code> with no gate and no pill would become the
  fourth invisible pointer.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Statute-overlap check</h2>
<p>
  An anchor-by-anchor pass over the platform-decisions statute found <strong>no anchor governing backlog item
  retirement</strong> — backlog lifecycle is explicitly delegated out to the backlog-workflow doc. Four
  anchors are adjacent and <em>compose</em> rather than collide: the <strong>backlog hold model</strong>
  pointer (which governs holds, not exits, and is the codified form of the "a park can't carry it" argument);
  <strong>ci-lifecycle-total-label-function</strong> (self-scoped to PR labels, but its principle — state is a
  positively-applied marker, never inferred from an absence — is the precedent for Finding 2);
  <strong>faithful-derivation-exclude-not-fabricate</strong> (supports excluding a folded item from the
  burndown rather than fabricating a delivered date); and
  <strong>state-lives-where-its-nature-dictates</strong> (a fold is durable shared repo truth, not session
  intent, so it belongs in committed frontmatter — which also explains why the retreat from a
  <code>preparing</code> status to a local uncommitted hold is not a precedent against a folded status: that
  hold is transient, a fold is permanent).
</p>

<h2>Recommendation</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Retire the folded duplicate.</strong> 5/5 prior art, and the "stay open" branch's own stated
  mechanism — whoever claims the survivor resolves the sibling in the same pass as delivered work — commits
  the exact burndown falsification the question was opened to avoid.</li>
  <li><strong>Carry it as a new <code>folded</code> status</strong> plus a required pointer to the survivor and
  a fold date — the reference-retirement triplet shape, adapted. It is near-free in the loader, it carries a
  positive marker per Finding 2, and the enum objection is scope-bound.</li>
  <li><strong>Ship the unfold path in the same slice.</strong> An irreversible fold is the one failure mode
  prior art actually recorded.</li>
</ul>

